</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Sasaki</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Telesurveillance system using television operating state for elderly persons living alone. 2005</article-title><year>2005</year><conf-name>Proceedings of Joint Meeting of International Workshop on E-health and 2nd International Conference on Ubiquitous Healthcare</conf-name><conf-date>2005 Nov 1-3</conf-date><conf-loc>Aizu Fukushima Japan</conf-loc></citation></ref><ref id="R7"><label>7</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Suzuki</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Ogawa</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Otake</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Rhythm of daily living and detection of atypical days for elderly people living alone as determined with a monitoring system</article-title><source>J Telemed Telecare</source><year>2006</year><volume>12</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>208</fpage><lpage>214</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16774704</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R8"><label>8</label><citation citation-type="confproc"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Mizukura</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Kimura</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Tamura</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Improving personal health through the home healthcare model verification project in Osaka Japan. 2005</article-title><year>2005</year><conf-name>Proceedings of Joint Meeting of International Workshop on E-health and 2nd International Conference on Ubiquitous Healthcare </conf-name><conf-date>2005 Nov.1-3 </conf-date><conf-loc>Aizu Fukushima Japan</conf-loc></citation></ref><ref id="R9"><label>9</label><citation citation-type="confproc"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Watsuji</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The home healthcare project in Japan&#x02014;the introduction of the system development and the field test 2005</article-title><year>2005</year><conf-name>Proceedings of Joint Meeting of International Workshop on E-health and 2nd International Conference on Ubiquitous Healthcare</conf-name><conf-date> 2005 Nov.1-3 </conf-date><conf-loc>Aizu Fukushima Japan</conf-loc></citation></ref><ref id="R10"><label>10</label><citation citation-type="other"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Tamura</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A smart house for emergencies in the elderly</article-title><source>Assistive Technology Research Series 19 Smart Homes and Beyond</source><year>2006</year><fpage>7</fpage><lpage>11</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="R11"><label>11</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Nambu</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Nakajima</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Noshiro</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Tamura</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>An algorithm for the automatic detection of health conditions</article-title><source>IEEE Eng Med Biol Mag</source><year>2005</year><volume>24</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>38</fpage><lpage>42</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16119211</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Fig. (1)</label><caption><p>Measuring unit.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TOMINFOJ-2-49_F1"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Fig. (2)</label><caption><p>Circuit diagram of electric field sensor.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TOMINFOJ-2-49_F2"/></fig><fig id="F3" position="float"><label>Fig. (3)</label><caption><p>Experimental setup for behavioral monitoring. Four sensor units and one data storage terminal are installed.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TOMINFOJ-2-49_F3"/></fig><fig id="F4" position="float"><label>Fig. (4)</label><caption><p>Radio communication range. A sender unit transmits 50 packets at each distance from the data storage terminal, and packet communication success rate is calculated.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TOMINFOJ-2-49_F4"/></fig><fig id="F5" position="float"><label>Fig. (5)</label><caption><p>Environmental noise and sensor output.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TOMINFOJ-2-49_F5"/></fig><fig id="F6" position="float"><label>Fig. (6)</label><caption><p>Signal processing of general-purpose sensor (microwave oven). The received signal mainly comprises a 50 Hz component derived from the commercial electric power system.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TOMINFOJ-2-49_F6"/></fig><fig id="F7" position="float"><label>Fig. (7)</label><caption><p>Signal processing of television sensor. Received signal mainly consists of 15.7 kHz pulses which come from electromagnet for horizontal scanning of CRT.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TOMINFOJ-2-49_F7"/></fig><fig id="F8" position="float"><label>Fig. (8)</label><caption><p>Experimental result of television on/off test.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TOMINFOJ-2-49_F8"/></fig><fig id="F9" position="float"><label>Fig. (9)</label><caption><p>Time-course of setting the threshold. At the beginning of the experiment, the target appliance is in the power-off state. By pressing the first button, the sensor output (noise) is recorded. The appliance is then powered on and the threshold value is adjusted by pressing the second button.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TOMINFOJ-2-49_F9"/></fig><fig id="F10" position="float"><label>Fig. (10)</label><caption><p>Experimental results of the proposed system (electric appliance usage monitoring).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TOMINFOJ-2-49_F10"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Specifications of the Measuring Unit</p></caption><table frame="border" rules="all" width="100%"><thead><tr><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Item</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Specification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Processor</bold></td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">PIC16F876 (10 MHz, Microchip Technology Inc.)</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Radio module</bold></td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">CDC-TR02B (315 MHz, 115.2 kbps, amplitude shift keying, Design Circuit Inc.)</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Antenna</bold></td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">RH-3 (1/4-&#x003bb;, Diamond)</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Network structure</bold></td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Ad-hoc (CSMA)</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Target (resonance) frequency</bold></td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">50/60 Hz for general-purpose sensor, 15.7 kHz for television</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Unit size (H &#x000d7; W &#x000d7; D)</bold></td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">100 &#x000d7; 95 &#x000d7; 120 mm</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T2" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Definitions of Conventional Television Formats</p></caption><table frame="border" rules="all" width="100%"><thead><tr><th rowspan="2" colspan="1">Item</th><th colspan="2" rowspan="1">Format</th></tr><tr><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">NTSC, PAL-M, PAL 60</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">PAL, SECAM</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Scanning line <italic>N </italic>[lines]</bold></td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">525</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">625</td></tr><tr><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Frame rate <italic>f<sub>V</sub></italic> [Hz]</bold></td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">29.97</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">25</td></tr><tr><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Horizontal scanning frequency <italic>f<sub>H</sub></italic> [kHz]</bold></td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15.734</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15.625</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T3" position="float"><label>Table 3</label><caption><p>Output Voltage of General-Purpose Sensor (Microwave Ovens)</p></caption><table frame="border" rules="all" width="100%"><tbody><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Year of Manufacture</bold></td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1994</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1996</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1997</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Rated Power Consumption [W]</bold></td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">960</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">920</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1000</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Average Sensor Output [V]</bold></td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.86</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.18</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.00</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T4" position="float"><label>Table 4</label><caption><p>Dependence of Sensor Output (Television Sensor) on Screen Size</p></caption><table frame="border" rules="all" width="100%"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Screen Size [in]</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">14</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">24</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">27</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">33</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Year of Manufacture</bold></td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1995</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1995</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1989</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1989</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Rated Power Consumption [W]</bold></td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">67</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">131</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">153</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">169</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Average Sensor Output [V]</bold></td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.01</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.01</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.97</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.92</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></floats-wrap></article>